<worksheet>
    <title>LT 6 Linear Regression Hot Dog Style</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            LT 6: Given a data set, make a linear regression model using Desmos, identify and explain the meaning of the slope in the context, identify and explain the meaning of the y-intercept in the context, find inputs and outputs and explain their meaning.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <page>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                The table below gives the number of hot dogs <m>H</m>, that a competitive hot dog eater has eaten, <m>t</m> minutes after the competition began.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.01in">
            <statement>
                <tabular top="minor" bottom="medium" left="major" right="major">
                    <col width="50%" halign="center"/>
                    <col width="50%" halign="center"/>
                    <row halign="center">
                        <cell><m>t=</m> time in minutes</cell>
                        <cell><m>H=</m> number of hot dogs eaten</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row halign="center">
                        <cell>0</cell>
                        <cell>8</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row halign="center">
                        <cell>3</cell>
                        <cell>22</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row halign="center">
                        <cell>5</cell>
                        <cell>36</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row halign="center">
                        <cell>7</cell>
                        <cell>56</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row halign="center">
                        <cell>9</cell>
                        <cell>64</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row halign="center">
                        <cell>10</cell>
                        <cell>71</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row halign="center">
                        <cell>15</cell>
                        <cell>106</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
                <p>
                    Is the data perfectly linear? If it is, find the equation. If it is not, find the regression equation.
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Go to Desmos.com and click on the + sign at the top left, and choose Table.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Enter the time under <m>x_1</m> and the number of hot dogs under <m>y_1</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            In the top left corner of the table row is an icon that looks like a line with dots on either side. Click on this icon.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Below the table you will see a linear equation which includes a lot of decimal places. Round the equation to 2 decimal places and write the regression equation in function notation using the variables from the table.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <answer>
                    <m>H(t)=6.66t+5.21</m>
                </answer>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.01in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the slope and it meaning in the context of this problem.  Remember, slope is a rate.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    The slope 6.66.  This means that according to the model, 6.66 hot dogs are eaten per minute.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.01in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the <m>H</m>-intercept and its meaning in the context of this problem?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    The <m>H</m>-intercept is (0, 5.21).  This means that according to the model, 5.21 hot dogs had been eaten before time began.  This is a model breakdown because no hot dogs could be consumed before a competition began.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.01in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the regression model equation to evaluate <m>H(3)</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Is this value the same as the value you given in the table of data?  Why or why not?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>H(3)=25.19</m>.  The table output is 22.  The model equation output is different because it is an equation that best fits all the data.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    True or False: When working with a set of data and its regression equation, the data is always the correct answer.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    False.  The data is only used to generate the regression model equation.  All answers are found using the regression model, even if it is a data input.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </task>
    </exercise>
    </page>
    <page>
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    We want to find the number of hot dogs eaten in 20 minutes.  What would the function notation look like for this problem?
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>H(20)</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <task workspace="0.01in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Show your work in finding the answer to previous question.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <m>H(20)=138.41</m> It approximately 138 hot dogs are eaten in 20 minutes.
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise workspace="0.01in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    According to the model, how many minutes will it take to eat 100 hot dogs?  Show your work using a different method, and round to the nearest whole.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    It will take approximately 14 minutes to eat 100 hot dogs.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise >
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Consider the domain:
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What does the domain represent in this story; minutes or number of hot dogs eaten?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    Minutes.
                </p>
            </answer>
            <task workspace="0.01in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Does infinity make sense for the situation of a hot dog eating competition?  Why or why not?
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <answer>
                    <p>
                        No.  A competition will not go on indefinitely.  The contestants will come to a point that they cannot eat anymore hot dogs, or the competition will be timed.  Negative time does not exist, so negative infinity would not make sense.
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </task>
            <task workspace="0.01in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What is the practical domain for this story?  Answer in interval or inequality notation, and explain what it means in the context of the problem.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    Answer can vary.  One example: [0,20] or <m>0 \le t \le 20</m>  Which means the competition goes for 20 minutes.
                </p>
            </answer>
            </task>
            <task workspace="0.01in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Consider the practical domain you chose.  Use the domain values to help you find the practical range and explain what it means.  Show work and round values to the nearest whole.  Answer in the same notation you used for the domain.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <answer>
                    <p>
                        [5,138] or <m>5 \le H \le 138</m> According to the model equation, 5 hot dogs were eaten when time began, and 138 hot dogs eaten at 20 minutes.
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </task>
        </exercise>
        
    </page>
</worksheet>